      "lean_guess": "The argument proceeds in three steps. Step 1: Show that the Collatz halting problem (given n, does T^k(n) = 1 for some k?) is Pi^0_2-complete. This means it is at least as hard as any Pi^0_2 statement, including Con(PA). Step 2: Show that if Collatz is false (there exists n whose orbit diverges or cycles), then Con(PA) implies a contradiction. This would mean not-Collatz implies not-Con(PA), i.e., Collatz follows from Con(PA). Step 3: Show that if Collatz is true, then PA cannot prove it, because the proof would require a consistency-strength beyond PA. The key: the Collatz map can simulate any Turing machine. Specifically, Conway (1972) showed that a generalization of the Collatz map (the 'Fractran' language) is Turing-complete. The specific 3n+1 map is not known to be Turing-complete, but it is conjectured that the set of Collatz orbits encodes the set of PA-proofs. If this is the case, then Collatz is equivalent to Con(PA). Test: formalize the Fractran encoding of Turing machines in Lean 4, show that the 3n+1 map can simulate a register machine, and establish the reduction from Collatz to Con(PA).",
